Show an on-screen indicator while a recording is running.

Upstream gives no visual sign that recording is active. CMD_EVENT_RECORDING_TOGGLE
calls RECORD_INIT or RECORD_DEINIT and neither pushes a message; record_driver.c
contains no runloop_msg_queue_push at all, and MSG_RECORDING_TO -- the one string
that sounds like a notification -- is used only inside RARCH_LOG. There is no
notification_show_recording setting because there is nothing to gate. So the only
evidence a recording is running is a line in a log file, which on a handheld with
no tally light leaves no way to answer "am I still recording?".

Drawn inside gfx_widgets_frame rather than as a compositor overlay, and that is the
whole point of doing it here. The alternative was a second Wayland surface from our
own OSD process, but a permanently mapped surface can cost the compositor its
direct-scanout path and force full GPU composition of every frame -- a real cost on
this Mali G52, and it would appear as the game running worse for the entire length
of a recording. Inside this pass it is one quad in a render that already happens.

It does not appear in the recording. Capture taps the core framebuffer before this
pass, which is the same reason shaders are not captured.

Sized from simple_widget_height so it follows the widget scale rather than being a
fixed pixel size that looks wrong on a 940x720 panel, and placed top left because
the status text (FPS, framecount, memory) already owns the top right. It pulses on
a triangle wave off the frame counter so it reads as active rather than as a dead
pixel, with no wall-clock dependency and no float math beyond one divide.

Round, and drawn as horizontal scanlines because gfx_display_draw_quad is the only
primitive available here: there is no circle or polygon helper, and the widget icon
set is functional glyphs (pause, rewind, check) with no plain disc, so a texture
would add a runtime dependency on asset PNGs being installed. One quad per row, so
a 16px dot is 16 tiny quads. The rows deliberately do not overlap -- overlapping
alpha would compound and blotch the edges.

diff --git a/gfx/gfx_widgets.c b/gfx/gfx_widgets.c
index 17d4b94189..0cdc0e93a5 100644
--- a/gfx/gfx_widgets.c
+++ b/gfx/gfx_widgets.c
@@ -38,6 +38,7 @@
 #include "../configuration.h"
 #include "../file_path_special.h"
 #include "../msg_hash.h"
+#include "../record/record_driver.h"
 
 #include "../tasks/task_content.h"
 #include "../tasks/tasks_internal.h"
@@ -1688,6 +1689,92 @@ void gfx_widgets_frame(void *data)
    }
 #endif
 
+   /* Recording indicator (Leaf).
+    *
+    * Upstream has no on-screen sign that a recording is running: the toggle
+    * calls RECORD_INIT/RECORD_DEINIT and neither pushes a message, so the only
+    * evidence is a line in the log. On a handheld with no tally light that
+    * leaves no way to answer "am I still recording?".
+    *
+    * Drawn here rather than as a compositor overlay on purpose. A second
+    * permanently-mapped Wayland surface can cost the compositor its
+    * direct-scanout path and force full GPU composition of every frame, which
+    * would slow the game down for the whole recording. Inside this pass it is
+    * one quad in a render that already runs.
+    *
+    * It does NOT appear in the clip: recording taps the core framebuffer before
+    * this pass, which is the same reason shaders are not captured.
+    *
+    * Sized from simple_widget_height so it follows the widget scale instead of
+    * being a fixed pixel size that is wrong on a small panel, and placed top
+    * left because the status text (FPS, memory) owns the top right.
+    *
+    * Round, and built from horizontal scanlines because gfx_display_draw_quad is
+    * the only primitive available here -- there is no circle or polygon helper,
+    * and the widget icon set is functional glyphs (pause, rewind, check) with no
+    * plain disc, so using a texture would add a runtime dependency on asset PNGs
+    * being installed. One quad per row, so a ~16px dot costs 16 tiny quads; the
+    * rows do not overlap, which matters because overlapping alpha would compound
+    * and blotch the edges. */
+   {
+      recording_state_t *rec_st = recording_state_get_ptr();
+
+      if (rec_st && rec_st->enable)
+      {
+         static float rec_dot_color[16] = COLOR_HEX_TO_FLOAT(ICON_COLOR_RED, 1.0f);
+         unsigned dot_size              = p_dispwidget->simple_widget_height / 3;
+         unsigned dot_pad               = p_dispwidget->simple_widget_padding;
+         /* Pulse so it reads as active rather than as a dead pixel or a stuck
+          * overlay. Triangle wave off the frame counter: no float math beyond a
+          * divide, and no dependency on wall-clock time. ~2s at 60fps. */
+         unsigned phase                 = (unsigned)(p_dispwidget->gfx_widgets_frame_count % 120);
+         float pulse                    = (phase < 60)
+            ? ((float)phase / 60.0f)
+            : ((float)(120 - phase) / 60.0f);
+         float radius;
+         unsigned row;
+
+         if (dot_size < 6)
+            dot_size = 6;
+
+         radius = (float)dot_size / 2.0f;
+
+         gfx_display_set_alpha(rec_dot_color, 0.35f + (0.65f * pulse));
+
+         for (row = 0; row < dot_size; row++)
+         {
+            /* Row centre relative to the circle centre, then the chord half-width
+             * at that height. +0.5 samples the middle of the row rather than its
+             * top edge, which keeps the top and bottom rows symmetric. */
+            float dy      = ((float)row + 0.5f) - radius;
+            float inside  = (radius * radius) - (dy * dy);
+            float half_w;
+            unsigned w;
+
+            if (inside <= 0.0f)
+               continue;
+
+            half_w = (float)sqrt((double)inside);
+            w      = (unsigned)((half_w * 2.0f) + 0.5f);
+
+            if (w == 0)
+               continue;
+
+            gfx_display_draw_quad(
+                  p_disp,
+                  userdata,
+                  video_width, video_height,
+                  (int)((float)dot_pad + radius - half_w),
+                  (int)(dot_pad + row),
+                  w, 1,
+                  video_width, video_height,
+                  rec_dot_color,
+                  NULL
+                  );
+         }
+      }
+   }
+
    /* Status Text (FPS, framecount, memory, core status message) */
    if (     fps_show
          || framecount_show
